Plan mode makes the writing tools refuse. Some helpers — full code subagents — are offered only with Projects access, not in Plan and not on sponsored runs.

== How do I start? ==
# Open [https://freebuff.com freebuff.com] and download Desktop, or open the web app.
# Or: <code>npm install -g freebuff</code>, then run <code>freebuff</code> inside a project folder.
# Open an existing project, or start an empty folder.
# Ask something small first: ''"Explain what this project does."''
# Then one step at a time, checking the diff as you go.

'''Brief it like a ticket:'''
* '''Outcome''' — what is true when the task is done.
* '''Boundary''' — what it may touch and what it must leave alone.
* '''Check''' — the command, test or URL that proves it.

== How much does it cost? ==
Freebuff is funded by '''text ads''', not a subscription; paid plans add more model time.

* Balances are shown in '''hours''' since 2026-10-15. '''1 hour = 5 Freebucks.''' Freebucks still appear as the credit unit.
* A model costs hours at the rate in the picker: one-hour model = one hour of balance, three-hour model = three, free experimental models = none.
* The free allowance depends on where you are: 30 hours a day in the United States, 21 or 12 in the countries listed there, 5 elsewhere, and 4 on a VPN or proxy. The full table is on the [[Freebuff Wiki#How much does it cost?|Home page]], and the daily pool is spent before your wallet.
* Referrals and bounties earn extra hours, which go into a wallet and are used after the daily allowance.

See the [[Details|Details page]] for the model list, plan bonuses and the glossary.

== What are its limits? ==
* Vague requests give vague results.
* It will not type your passwords or log in for you.
* No OS sandbox on Desktop: commands use your user's rights and can reach beyond the opened folder. Administrator rights are never granted implicitly.
* Unknown projects can carry executable settings; the CLI asks before trusting project-level agent or MCP configuration, and trust is tied to a directory.
* A green build is evidence about the build, not proof the feature is right.
* Plan and Build are cleanly separated on Desktop; the CLI ties the root agent to the selected model and an internal lite mode, so confirm behaviour instead of assuming.
* Cloud chats keep separate copies of files; merging is yours, through git.
* Free hours are limited per day.
* Context is finite: long chats get compressed into a summary, so restate hard requirements.

== Common questions ==
; Do I need to know programming?
: No, but clear requests and a checkable result matter more than vocabulary.

; Is it really free?
: Ads pay for the models. No card and no API key; paid plans add hours.

; Which is better, Desktop or Cloud?
: Desktop for your own local project with no sandbox; Cloud for a GitHub repo you want previewed and deployed.

; Can it push to my repository?
: It is instructed not to commit, push or open a pull request unless you ask — that is a rule, not a technical lock, so review before approving.

; Does it train on my code?
: Only where a model or feature says data may be used for training. Separately, usage data may train ad-selection models; project files and connected repos are not given to ad providers.

; What is base3?
: The name of Freebuff's main agent loop: one agent investigates, edits and verifies with its own tools.

